-- title:
--   Lemma 4.2: $(J^1)'\le(J^2)'$ implies $(V^1_t)'\le(V^2_t)'$ and $a^1_t\le a^2_t$
-- statement:
--   Under the §4 standing assumptions, let $J^1,J^2$ satisfy H Hypothesis, let $V^n_t$ be defined by (4.10)–(4.11) from $J^n$, and let $a^n_t(x)$ be the optimizer of (4.11) for $V^n_t$, $t=0,\dots,N-1$. Assume $(J^1)'\le(J^2)'$. Then for $t=0,\dots,N-1$ and $x\ge0$,
--   $$(V^1_t)'(x)\le(V^2_t)'(x)\qquad\text{and}\qquad a^1_t(x)\le a^2_t(x).\quad(4.16)$$
--
--   This comparison principle drives the monotonicity of the fixed-point map in the proof of Theorem 4.1.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conclusion holds for almost every realization $y$ of the common noise, all $t$ and all $x\ge0$; at $x=0$ the derivative is the right derivative.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: V_t from (4.10)–(4.11) → valueENN: the supremum over a ≥ 0 of the kernel conditional expectation of V_{t+1}(x + a) minus C_t(a), in [0, ∞]; value is its real part; reason: the supremum in [0, ∞] has no junk value; conditional expectations as in the kernel encoding.
--   - encoding: a_t(x) the optimizer for V_t(x) → any functions a_t(x, y) ≥ 0 attaining the supremum in (4.11) with a finite objective, for a.e. y, every t < N and x ≥ 0 (IsOptimizer); reason: the optimizer is unique (C_t strictly convex), so this covers the paper's optimizer without choosing it.
--   - encoding: (J¹)′ ≤ (J²)′ on ℝ₊ → at every x > 0 and every y; reason: at x = 0 the footnote-2 derivatives are limits from x > 0, so the condition at x = 0 follows.
--   - reading: statements about the random functions V_t, a_t (F^Y_t-measurable) without a printed "a.s." → for a.e. y, simultaneously for all t < N and x ≥ 0; reason: V_t and a_t are random variables determined up to null sets; "for all x at once" is the stronger reading.
--   - reading: (V_t)′(x), x ∈ ℝ₊ → rderiv: right derivative at x = 0, ordinary derivative at x > 0; asserted for every x ≥ 0; reason: footnote 2 defines V′(0) as a limit; for t ≤ N − 1 it is finite by (4.15), and including x = 0 is the stronger reading.
--   - encoding: periods k = 0, …, N − 1 with noises X_{k+1}, Y_{k+1}; F^Y_k = σ(Y_1, …, Y_k) → Lean coordinates X k, Y k (0-based); F^Y_k is generated by the k-prefix of the noise path (truncPath); reason: Lean convention; same instances.
--   - generalization: the standing model of §3/§4 also fixes the idiosyncratic noise X (independent of Y, X_1, …, X_N independent) and φ_k with (3.1) → no assumption on X or φ; reason: X and φ do not occur in the statement, which therefore specializes to the standing model.
--   - encoding: (3.6) of H Hypothesis, an inequality between random variables 'for all x ≥ 0, k' → almost surely, simultaneously for all x ≥ 0 and k (Bound36); reason: x ↦ J(x, y) is continuous on ℝ₊ (H Hypothesis (1)) and the costs C_k are C¹, so 'for each x, a.s.' and 'a.s., for all x' are the same condition; same instances.
-- source:
--   Zhou, Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution games, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), Lemma 4.2, p.22, (4.16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_Setup
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_HHypothesis
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_ValueRecursion

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace RelPerfMFG

/-- **Lemma 4.2**, Zhou, *Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution
games*, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), p.22. Let `Jⁿ : ℝ₊ × (ℝᵉ)ᴺ → ℝ₊` satisfy H Hypothesis for
`n = 1, 2`. Define `Vⁿ_t` via (4.10) and (4.11) w.r.t. `Jⁿ` for `t = 0, …, N`, and let `aⁿ_t(x)` be
the optimizer for `Vⁿ_t` for `t = 0, …, N − 1`. Assume `(J¹)′ ≤ (J²)′`. Then for
`t = 0, …, N − 1`, `(V¹_t)′(x) ≤ (V²_t)′(x)` and `a¹_t(x) ≤ a²_t(x)` (4.16).

**Formalization Note.** §4 standing assumptions on `μ`, `Y`, `C_t`. `Vⁿ_t = value` (the real
value of `valueENN`), `′` is `rderiv` (right derivative at `x = 0`). The optimizers are any
functions attaining the supremum in (4.11) (`IsOptimizer`). `(J¹)′ ≤ (J²)′` is assumed at every
`x > 0` and every `y` (at `0` it follows by the limit of footnote 2). The conclusion holds for
a.e. `y` (the `V_t` are random variables), all `t < N` and all `x ≥ 0`. 0-based periods. -/
theorem lemma_4_2 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {N e : ℕ}
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (Y : Ω → NoisePath N e) (hY : Measurable Y)
    (C : Fin N → ℝ → ℝ) (hC : IsCostS4 C)
    (J₁ J₂ : ℝ → NoisePath N e → ℝ) (hJ₁ : HHypothesis μ Y C J₁) (hJ₂ : HHypothesis μ Y C J₂)
    (a₁ a₂ : ℕ → ℝ → NoisePath N e → ℝ)
    (ha₁ : IsOptimizer μ Y C J₁ a₁) (ha₂ : IsOptimizer μ Y C J₂ a₂)
    (hder : ∀ y, ∀ x : ℝ, 0 < x →
      rderiv (fun x => J₁ x y) x ≤ rderiv (fun x => J₂ x y) x) :
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, ∀ t : ℕ, t < N → ∀ x : ℝ, 0 ≤ x →
      rderiv (fun x => value μ Y C J₁ t x (Y ω)) x ≤
          rderiv (fun x => value μ Y C J₂ t x (Y ω)) x ∧
        a₁ t x (Y ω) ≤ a₂ t x (Y ω) := by sorry

end RelPerfMFG
